<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berlin Airlift</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Berlin Blockade</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> Airlift was the supply of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-berlin">West Berlin</a> by air in 1948–1949, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> blockaded road, rail and canal access to the city's western sectors in June 1948. American, British and other Western aircraft flew in food, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and other goods, at the peak landing a plane every few minutes. The Soviets lifted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> in May 1949, and the crisis hastened the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> into two states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berlin-airlift.jpg" alt="Berlin Airlift" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Ries / USAF · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C-54_landing_at_Tempelhof.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
